  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1201 AM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nMonday through Friday:\n\nInfluence from the upper-level ridge and surface high pressure \nover the Great Lakes will continue trough the holiday as it will\nbe reinforced by an additional area of high pressure building \ndown. Predominately easterly flow off of Lake Michigan will help\nkeep a couple of degree cooler than what we have been seeing \nalong with less humidity. Highs for Labor Day are looking to top\noff in the upper 70s to low 80s. \n\nThen influence from the high pressure wanes into Tuesday as the\nupper-level ridge axis slides eastward while and upper-level \ntrough lifts across the Upper Midwest. This pattern shift is \nexpected to bring more southerly flow to southern WI along with \nlow-level WAA, moisture advection, and temps warming back into \nthe 80s for Tuesday into Wednesday. This will also be the next \nwindow for increased shower and storm chances across the area as\nit brings a surface low across Manitoba/Ontario with a cold \nfront drifting across the Upper Midwest later Tuesday into \nWednesday. However, the better upper- level dynamics look to \nremain to the north closer tied to the trough/low itself, the \nlow-level forcing from the front along with some WAA and signs \nof a LLJ are looking to be enough for the increased PoP chances \nlater Tuesday into Wednesday. \n\nThen behind the midweek system, the pattern is trending more \nbenign with zonal upper-level flow and high pressure building \nacross the region for the end of the week. \n\nWagner",
